'''''Hypericum grandifolium''''', the '''large-leaved St John's wort''', is a species of [[flowering plant]] in the family [[Hypericaceae]]. The plant is a bushy [[shrub]] that can grow to almost 2 meters tall. It has large leaves, golden yellow [[petal]]s, and [[Capsule (fruit)|seed capsules]] that split open. ''H.&nbsp;grandifolium'' is native to the [[Canary Islands]] and [[Madeira]] in [[Macaronesia]], but has become [[Invasive species|invasive]] in other regions, including [[California]], after escaping from cultivation as an [[ornamental plant]]. It is [[Parasitism|parasitized]] by [[wasp]]s and [[Fungus|fungi]], and is capable of reproducing through its [[rhizome]]s.

First described in 1821 by [[Jacques Denys Choisy|Jacques Choisy]], the ambiguity of its original description meant that the species was often misidentified. Specimens were variously called ''H.&nbsp;elatum'', ''H.&nbsp;anglicum'', ''Androsaemum webbianum'', and others. The species was placed into section ''[[Hypericum sect. Androsaemum|Androsaemum]]'' of ''[[Hypericum]]'' by [[Norman Robson (botanist)|Norman Robson]] in 1984, and it is most closely related in appearance and classification to the other species in the section, especially {{nowrap|''[[Hypericum androsaemum|H. androsaemum]]''}}, {{nowrap|''[[Hypericum hircinum|H. hircinum]]''}}, and {{nowrap|''[[Hypericum × inodorum|H. × inodorum]]''}}. The species is not used in modern medical applications, but may have [[Anticarcinogen|anticancer]] potential and could be used to treat ear [[edema]]s.

== Etymology ==
The [[genus]] name ''Hypericum'' is possibly derived from the [[Greek language|Greek]] words {{Lang|el|hyper}} (above) and {{Lang|el|eikon}} (picture), in reference to the tradition of hanging the plant over religious [[icon]]s in the home.{{Sfn|Coombes|2012|p=172}} The [[Botanical name|specific epithet]] ''grandifolium'' is made of the Latin words {{Lang|la|grandis}} (large, full) and {{Lang|la|folium}} (leaf).<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Lewis |first1=Charlton |last2=Short |first2=Charles |date=1879 |title=A Latin Dictionary |url=https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0059%3Aalphabetic+letter%3DG%3Aentry+group%3D14%3Aentry%3Dgrandis |access-date=13 January 2023 |website=Perseus Tufts}}</ref> As such, it is called the large-leaved St John's wort in English.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |title=''Hypericum grandifolium'' Profile |url=https://www.cal-ipc.org/plants/profile/hypericum-grandifolium-profile/ |access-date=2024-01-14 |website=California Invasive Plant Council |language=}}</ref> ''Hypericum grandifolium'' is known as {{Lang|es|'''malfurada'''}},<ref group="note">Also spelled {{Lang|es|malforada}}, {{Lang|es|milfurada}}, {{Lang|es|malforado}}, or {{Lang|es|maljurada}}</ref>{{Sfn|Lowe|1868|p=75}} {{Lang|es|'''malfurada grande'''}}, and {{Lang|es|'''corazoncillo'''}} in Spanish, the language used where it is native.<ref name=":1">{{Cite news |last=Wrubel |first=Eric |date=2018 |title=Early Detection News |url=https://www.nps.gov/articles/early-detection-news-august-2018.htm |access-date=14 January 2024 |work=National Park Service}}</ref><ref name="Banco Datos">{{cite web |title=Biota – ''Hypericum grandifolium'' Choisy |url=https://www.biodiversidadcanarias.es/biota/especie/F00859 |website=Banco de Datos de Biodiversidad de Canarias (Canary Islands Biodiversity Data Bank) |publisher=Gobierno de Canarias (Government of the Canary Islands) |access-date=6 February 2024 |language=es}}</ref> The name {{Lang|es|corazoncillo}} is a [[diminutive]] form of the Spanish word for heart, {{Lang|es|corazon}}.<ref>{{cite book |last1=Macdonald |first1=George Robert |title=Spanish-English and English-Spanish Commercial Dictionary of the Words and Terms Used in Commercial Correspondence Which are Not Given in the Dictionaries in Ordinary Use; Compound Phrases, Idiomatic and Technical Expressions, etc |date=1918 |publisher=Pitman |location=London |page=71 |url=https://archive.org/details/spanishenglishen00macduoft/page/71 |access-date=6 February 2024}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=corazoncillo |url=https://dle.rae.es/corazoncillo |website=Diccionario de la lengua española (Dictionary of the Spanish Language) |publisher=Real Academia Española (Royal Spanish Academy) |access-date=7 February 2024 |language=es}}</ref>

== Description ==
[[File:Hypericum_grandifolium.jpg|left|thumb|Bushy form of the plant]]
''Hypericum grandifolium'' is a bushy shrub that grows 50–180 centimeters tall. Its branches generally grow upright or almost upright.{{Sfn|Robson|1984|p=298}} Across its distribution, different populations look almost identical and the species has minimal geographic variation in appearance.{{Sfn|Robson|1984|p=300}}

=== Vegetative structures ===
[[File:Hypericum_grandifolium_(Jardín_Botánico_Canario_Viera_y_Clavijo).jpg|left|thumb|Bark, stems, and leaves]]
When the plant is young, the [[Plant stem|stems]] are somewhat flattened and have four lines that run longitudinally up their length. When it matures, they become more cylindrical and have only two lines. There are [[Nodes (botany)|nodes]] with leaves every 0.2–0.4&nbsp;cm along the stem. The bark on the outside of the stem is scaly.{{Sfn|Robson|1984|p=298}}

The leaf blades are directly connected to the stem, and sometimes partially wrap around it. The blade is roughly 4–9&nbsp;cm long by 2.5–4.5&nbsp;cm wide and is a triangular-oval or oblong-oval shape. The end is blunt or rounded, and the base is flat or rounded. It is the same color on top and bottom and has a papery texture. There are four or five pairs of large lateral veins, and the leaf's network of tiny tertiary veins is visible on the top side. The leaf's [[Gland (botany)|glands]] are small, but they are found densely along its edges.{{Sfn|Robson|1984|p=298}}

=== Flowering structures ===
[[File:Hypericum_grandifolium_fruits_gomera_t_kvitvik_DSCN5075.JPG|left|thumb|Dried and splitting fruit]]
Each [[inflorescence|cluster of flowers]] has anywhere from 1–13 individual flowers. They come from two nodes on the stem that are separated by the same distance as the leaves are from each other. The flower cluster is shaped like a wide pyramid or closer to a [[corymb]], with the stems spreading out broadly. Sometimes there will be accessory flowers lower on the branches that carry the main clusters. Each flower is carried by a [[Pedicel (botany)|pedicel]] that is 0.5–1.1&nbsp;cm long. The leaf-like [[bract]]s may be the same size as other leaves, or could be smaller. They are elongated and may taper to a point.{{Sfn|Robson|1984|p=299}}

Each flower is 3–4.5&nbsp;cm wide. Before blossoming, the buds are an imperfect globe shape with an end that is blunt or rounded. The [[sepal]]s overlap one another and vary in size. They become larger during flowering and persist while the plant is fruiting. There are glands on the sepals in linear patterns and individual points; the glands are denser along the edges. The petals are golden yellow, measuring 1.8–2.2&nbsp;cm long and 0.6–0.8&nbsp;cm wide. The [[Stamen|stamens]] are grouped together in bundles of around 30–40, with the longest in the bundle being around 1.5–2.0&nbsp;cm long. The [[Style (botany)|styles]] grow upright and are slender, with a narrow [[Stigma (botany)|stigma]] that ends in a distinct head. The [[Capsule (fruit)|seed capsule]] is 0.8–1.3&nbsp;cm long with a leathery texture and a pointed end. After the plant fruits, the seed capsule entirely splits. The seeds inside are yellow-brown and around 0.12&nbsp;cm long, with a wing-like growth on one side and none on the ends.{{Sfn|Robson|1984|p=299}}

=== Similar species ===
''Hypericum grandifolium'' differs from both {{nowrap|''[[Hypericum androsaemum| H. androsaemum]]''}} and{{nowrap|''[[Hypericum hircinum| H. hircinum]]''}} by its bigger overall size and larger leaves. It also differs from{{nowrap|'' H. androsaemum''}} by having dry fruit (as opposed to a soft and fleshy berry), and from {{nowrap|''H. hircinum''}} by having sepals that do not fall off.<ref>{{Cite web |title=''Hypericum grandifolium'' {{small|Choisy}} |url=https://www.treesandshrubsonline.org/articles/hypericum/hypericum-grandifolium/ |access-date=2024-01-14 |website=Bean's Trees and Shrubs Online}}</ref> It has at times been confused with ''[[Hypericum × inodorum]]'' by some botanists,{{Sfn|Robson|1984|p=300}} which is an intermediate form of{{nowrap|'' H. androsaemum''}} and {{nowrap|''H. hircinum''}}.{{Sfn|Robson|1968|p=263}}

=== Chemistry ===
''Hypericum grandifolium'' produces several [[secondary metabolite]]s in detectable quantities. [[Hyperoside]] and [[quercitrin]] are found in major concentrations; [[chlorogenic acid]], [[isoquercitrin]], and [[quercetin]] are present in smaller concentrations.{{Sfn|Zorzetto|Sánchez-Mateo|Rabanal|Lupidi|2015|p=99}} The species also has a diverse [[essential oil]] profile. [[Nonane]] and various [[caryophyllene]]s are by far the most prolific oils,{{Sfn|Zorzetto|Sánchez-Mateo|Rabanal|Lupidi|2015|p=100}} but there is a wide array of compounds in more minute concentrations. These are mostly [[alkane]]s and [[sesquiterpene]] [[hydrocarbon]]s.{{Sfn|Zorzetto|Sánchez-Mateo|Rabanal|Lupidi|2015|p=102}}

== Taxonomy ==
''Hypericum grandifolium'' was first formally [[Species description|described]] by [[Jacques Denys Choisy]] in 1821.<ref>{{Cite web |title=''Hypericum grandifolium'' {{small|Choisy}} |url=http://powo.science.kew.org/taxon/urn:lsid:ipni.org:names:433467-1 |access-date=2024-01-14 |website=Plants of the World Online |publisher=Kew Science |language=en}}</ref> He collected the species' [[Type (biology)|type specimen]] on the island of [[Tenerife]].{{Sfn|Robson|1984|p=298}} This description was one of the first that dealt with [[Macaronesia]]n flora spanning multiple island groups.{{sfn|Hooker|1857|p=378}} In it, Choisy gave {{nowrap|'' H. grandifolium''}} the following brief identifying description:{{Sfn|Choisy|1821|p=38}}<ref group="note">Translation by [[GPT-3|GPT-3.5]]</ref>{{Text and translation|''Hypericum'' caule tereti, foliis magnis amplexicaulibus, calyce in pedunculum reflexo, corolla sublineari. Caulis fruticosus, erectus, rubricans, teres, ramosus; rami obliqui. |''Hypericum'' with a cylindrical stem, large embracing leaves, calyx bent back on the peduncle, and sublinear corolla. The stem is shrubby, erect, reddening, cylindrical, and branching; the branches are oblique.}}{{cladogram|{{Clade|style=font-size:100%;line-height:100%;width:300px;
