} was most closely related to ''Hypericum hircinum'' and ''Hypericum foliosum''.{{sfn|Meseguer|Aldasoro|Sanmartín|2013|p=386}}

== Distribution, habitat, and ecology ==
The native distribution of ''Hypericum grandifolium'' are the Spanish [[Canary Islands]] and the Portuguese island of [[Madeira]]. The species is also a garden plant, and has become [[Invasive species|invasive]] after escaping from [[Horticulture|cultivation]] in non-native areas. For example, it is invasive and spreading in the [[San Francisco Bay Area]], forming dense [[Forest stand|stands]] that force out native plants.<ref name=":0" /> It was first recorded in California in 2010, and is suspected to be harmful to the native ecology by the [[California Invasive Plant Council]].''<ref name=":1" />'' {{nowrap|'' H. grandifolium''}} has become [[Naturalisation (biology)|naturalized]] in parts of Australia and in Chile,<ref name=":0" /> but is listed as a weed in other parts of Australia like [[South Australia]] and [[Victoria (state)|Victoria]].<ref name=":1" />

The species can be found at elevations of 200–1,500 meters on the Canary Islands and 400–500 on Madeira. Its habitat is among evergreen forests like those of ''[[Laurus]]'' trees. It is also present on stony hillsides and cliffsides.{{Sfn|Robson|1984|p=299}} In areas that have been deforested by [[wildfire]], ''Hypericum grandifolium'' is a colonizing species that grows in the years following the disturbance.{{Sfn|Arevalo|Peraza|Alvarez|2008|p=6}} It often grows alongside the shrub ''[[Ageratina adenophora]]'' in California, where both species are invasive.<ref name=":1" />

''Hypericum grandifolium'' is a host to several [[Parasitism|parasites]]. These include the [[wasp]] ''[[Euderomphale gomer]]'', the [[Rust (fungus)|rust fungus]] ''[[Melampsora hypericorum]]'',<ref>{{Cite web |title=''Hypericum grandifolium'' {{small|Choisy}} |url=https://eol.org/pages/5707622 |access-date=2024-01-14 |website=Encyclopedia of Life}}</ref> and rarely the honey fungus ''[[Armillaria]]''.''<ref name=":2">{{Cite web |title=''Hypericum grandifolium'' |url=http://www.rhs.org.uk/plants/9006/hypericum-grandifolium/details |access-date=2024-01-14 |website=Royal Horticultural Society |language=en-gb}}</ref>'' {{nowrap|''H. grandifolium''}} is capable of reproducing through its [[rhizome]]s, and can also form thickets from its [[rootstock]].<ref name=":0" /> It is possible to [[Plant propagation|propagate]] {{nowrap|'' H. grandifolium''}} form either softwood or semi-ripe [[Cutting (plant)|cuttings]].<ref name=":2" />

== Research ==
The [[methanol]]-water extracts of ''Hypericum grandifolium'' exhibit moderate ''[[in vitro]]'' [[Anticarcinogen|anticancer]] potential against human tumor cells that was demonstrated in laboratory testing, and among ''Hypericum'' species native to the Canary Islands {{nowrap|''H. grandifolium''}} is the most effective in this regard. The plant's extracts were particularly effective at inducing [[cell death]] of [[HeLa]] cervical cancer.{{Sfn|Lacret|Puerta|Granica|Gonzalez-Bakker|2022|p=6101}}{{Sfn|Kladar|Srđenović|Grujić|5=2015|p=67}} When compared to ''[[Hypericum canariense]]'' and ''[[Hypericum reflexum]]'', the species showed the least amount of [[antioxidant]] and [[antimicrobial]] activity. It had no impact on [[gram-negative bacteria]] and only minimal impact on [[gram-positive bacteria]].{{Sfn|Zorzetto|Sánchez-Mateo|Rabanal|Lupidi|2015|p=105}} However, its essential oil extracts have demonstrated effectiveness in reducing ear and paw [[edema]]s.{{Sfn|Bonkanka|Smelcerovic|Zuehlke|Rabanal|2008|p=719}} A review of the medicinal properties of various ''Hypericum'' species in 2015 stated that the species was one of several that was effective against [[Drug resistance|drug-resistant]] bacteria, especially ''[[Enterococcus faecalis]]''.{{Sfn|Kladar|Srđenović|Grujić|5=2015|p=63}} In laboratory mice, the plant induced [[antidepressant]]-like effects, likely a result of its flavonoids and benzophenones,{{Sfn|Sanchez-Mateo|Bonkanka|Rabanal|2009|p=297}}{{Sfn|Prado|Rabanal|Sanchez-Mateo|2002|p=743}} and has also demonstrated properties that restrict [[nociception]], which could reduce pain.{{Sfn|Bonkanka|Sanchez-Mateo|Rabanal|2011|p=122}}

